original:
\begin{abstract}
Regional myocardial dysfunction can be clinically important while global ventricular measures remain preserved. Routine cine cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) is widely available and supports retrospective motion and strain estimation, and what it records is changing image intensity and anatomy without labels for myocardial material points. Every estimator therefore adds assumptions to recover correspondence, and the question for a regional claim is which attributes of an abnormality, its magnitude, location, spatial extent and timing, are recovered within a tolerance appropriate to the intended use.

This critical narrative review compares feature-tracking, deformable-registration, biomechanical and learning-based estimators by their inputs, representations, priors and supervision, and groups the validation literature by the claim each target can support: numerical phantoms, tagging, displacement encoding with stimulated echoes (DENSE), scan--rescan studies, late gadolinium enhancement and clinical outcomes. Current evidence most directly supports cine-derived circumferential strain at whole-slice, global and segmental scales under matched definitions, including improved segmental agreement with DENSE after DENSE-supervised learning. Agreement is weaker for regional than global quantities and weakest for radial strain. The one independent test against a prescribed focal scar preserved slice-averaged circumferential strain better than radial strain without quantifying recovery of the scar region, and direct tests of focal boundary, extent and peak timing remain sparse.

Apparent contradictions resolve when representation capacity, estimation error and input sufficiency are separated and when estimands are matched. A defensible validation programme pre-specifies the measurement contract, combines known-motion tests with registered material-sensitive in-vivo comparisons, and ties each regional claim to the method, acquisition, population, scale and reference for which it was demonstrated.
\end{abstract}

revision:
\begin{abstract}
Regional myocardial dysfunction can be clinically important despite preserved global ventricular function. Routine cine cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) permits retrospective motion and strain estimation, but its changing image intensities and boundaries do not explicitly label myocardial material points. Regional interpretation therefore depends on the estimator's assumptions and on what its validation actually measures.

This critical narrative review compares feature tracking, registration, biomechanical and learning-based methods by their inputs, representations, priors and supervision. It separates evidence from known-motion tests, tagging or displacement encoding with stimulated echoes (DENSE), repeatability, tissue characterisation and clinical outcomes. Selected paired studies support circumferential-strain agreement at whole-slice, global and segmental scales under matched definitions. DENSE-supervised learning improved end-systolic cine--DENSE agreement, with residual individual differences and limited external validation. Radial measurements are particularly sensitive to product and definition. A prescribed-scar evaluation of DeepStrain on MRXCAT2.0 reported component-specific errors averaged over whole slices; those results do not quantify recovery within the scar. Clinical discrimination and prognosis add useful evidence, but do not establish material-motion accuracy.

A defensible regional claim should specify the acquisition, estimator, population, spatial scale, strain definition, reference and tolerance. Controlled known-motion tests and aligned material-sensitive in-vivo comparisons can probe abnormality magnitude, location, extent and timing; downstream studies address clinical relevance. Input information, representation, estimation and reference uncertainty can contribute jointly to error. Matching the claim to these complementary tests is more informative than treating any single agreement measure as general validation.
\end{abstract}

